  "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t+1)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}+3)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{t+1},8{t}^{3}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}},24{t}^{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =\u27e8-\\frac{1}{(1+1)^{2}},24({1}^{2})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-\\frac{1}{4},24\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
